1
00:00:03,695 --> 00:00:06,815
Hello, everyone! I'm mimi, an illustrator.

2
00:00:06,815 --> 00:00:15,333
This video is for people who seriously want to improve their drawing, rather than just draw for fun.

3
00:00:15,333 --> 00:00:22,966
I'll speak from my own experience. If you want to improve your illustrations, please watch until the end.

4
00:00:24,642 --> 00:00:32,883
Today's theme is “Draw with your brain, not your hands.” Of course, you can't draw with your brain alone.

5
00:00:32,888 --> 00:00:43,683
You have to practice with your hands. But how you use your brain while doing so matters too.

6
00:00:43,687 --> 00:00:52,566
Here are five rules for improving your illustrations, based on what I keep in mind when I draw.

7
00:00:52,567 --> 00:01:00,916
They aren't rules you must follow. I hope something in my approach helps you.

8
00:01:04,800 --> 00:01:13,599
Rule one: “Finish it before you start drawing.” What does that mean?

9
00:01:13,599 --> 00:01:21,400
Before I start an illustration, I try to picture the finished work in my mind.

10
00:01:21,404 --> 00:01:35,316
I might make the glasses stand out, use brush texture for an analog feel, or make light falling on a face the focus of a dark scene.

11
00:01:35,324 --> 00:01:43,966
I don't need to see every detail, but I decide roughly what I want the picture to show before I begin.

12
00:01:43,971 --> 00:01:56,250
Without any vision of the result, I tend to gamble that something good will emerge if I just keep drawing.

13
00:01:56,251 --> 00:02:05,450
That gamble sometimes works. I don't mean you must stick rigidly to the image you first imagined.

14
00:02:05,455 --> 00:02:12,175
If I realize a different approach works better while drawing, I change it.

15
00:02:12,175 --> 00:02:20,275
Having an initial vision lets me compare it with what I've actually made.

16
00:02:20,275 --> 00:02:29,633
The mental image is a reference point for decisions, rather than a blueprint I must follow exactly.

17
00:02:29,635 --> 00:02:37,283
That's why I try to finish the picture in my mind before moving my hands.

18
00:02:40,811 --> 00:02:48,610
Rule two may be the most important: “Don't draw what you don't understand.”

19
00:02:48,610 --> 00:02:57,610
I try to reduce the parts I draw without understanding them myself.

20
00:02:57,610 --> 00:03:09,360
When using a reference, I don't just copy a dark area. I ask why it looks dark.

21
00:03:09,360 --> 00:03:18,760
Is it a shadow caused by the face's angle and the light's direction? Does the plane change because of the underlying bone structure?

22
00:03:18,760 --> 00:03:27,366
With folds in clothing, I ask where the fabric is being pulled from and why the fold runs in that direction.

23
00:03:27,370 --> 00:03:32,410
In other words, I study how the reference works instead of copying its answer.

24
00:03:32,410 --> 00:03:38,400
I valued this approach in drawing when I was preparing for the Tokyo University of the Arts entrance exam.

25
00:03:38,403 --> 00:03:47,516
I studied sculpture at Tokyo University of the Arts. While preparing for the exam, I felt my drawing change.

26
00:03:47,523 --> 00:03:56,730
Before that, I tried to reproduce what I saw on the page: “It looks like this, so I'll draw it like this.”

27
00:03:56,730 --> 00:04:04,410
Then I began asking myself what the shapes I was drawing connected to.

28
00:04:04,410 --> 00:04:24,766
I thought about the underlying structure, changes in color, and contours. I drew almost as if I could touch the object and reconstruct it in clay from my drawing.

29
00:04:24,774 --> 00:04:34,294
My drawing changed considerably once I approached it that way, and I passed the entrance exam around then.

30
00:04:34,294 --> 00:04:45,166
I still try to apply that approach to illustrations. A skill from life drawing doesn't necessarily transfer immediately.

31
00:04:45,173 --> 00:04:52,386
You don't need to explain everything perfectly. I still draw many things intuitively.

32
00:04:52,893 --> 00:05:04,564
But I try to remove each “I don't understand it, but this looks convincing” decision and explain what I draw.

33
00:05:04,564 --> 00:05:16,115
The more I can explain, the more clearly I can communicate through the picture. I ask why I draw a shape that way instead of memorizing shapes that look good.

34
00:05:16,684 --> 00:05:26,600
That is what I mean by “drawing with your brain, not your hands.”

35
00:05:26,600 --> 00:05:31,192
Rule three: “Drill what you can't do into your head.” It sounds harsh.

36
00:05:32,207 --> 00:05:42,007
I don't mean blame yourself. Discovering something you can't do means finding a new skill you can gain.

37
00:05:42,577 --> 00:05:48,662
People often say drawing is something your body learns. Repetition matters.

38
00:05:49,137 --> 00:05:59,596
Once you've learned a skill, your hands can do much of it automatically. Let your brain focus on what your hands haven't learned yet.

39
00:06:00,443 --> 00:06:07,516
Instead of repeating what I'm good at, I focus on what I can't do yet.

40
00:06:08,603 --> 00:06:25,513
Maybe I lose the shape of a head in profile, muddy skin tones in backlight, or draw vague hands. Rather than saying “I'm just bad,” I name the specific problem.

41
00:06:26,607 --> 00:06:35,673
Sometimes I write these observations down so I'm aware of exactly what I can't do yet.

42
00:06:37,160 --> 00:06:43,907
On my next quick sketch or full illustration, I aim to improve that one thing.

43
00:06:44,612 --> 00:06:52,727
I said something similar about quick sketching in my earlier video about improving at drawing in three months.

44
00:06:52,727 --> 00:07:05,400
Instead of deciding to make ten sketches, I choose a purpose: studying how the pelvis relates to the body's tilt, or where the center of gravity is.

45
00:07:05,407 --> 00:07:21,033
I don't make the number of drawings my goal. I try to gain one skill from each drawing. Then a weakness becomes experience I haven't collected yet.

46
00:07:25,691 --> 00:07:28,868
Rule four: “Don't leave the drawing to your hands alone.”

47
00:07:29,731 --> 00:07:47,292
As I draw, I get absorbed in how cute a face looks or how well I painted a part. That is enjoyable, but too much immersion makes me lose objectivity.

48
00:07:47,292 --> 00:07:56,683
I also zoom out, flip the image horizontally, and step back to inspect it.

49
00:07:56,690 --> 00:08:10,283
Physical distance doesn't always create mental distance. I can zoom out and still think, “That face is cute,” with the same excitement.

50
00:08:10,284 --> 00:08:20,033
So I imagine another, slightly stricter version of myself watching from behind while I draw.

51
00:08:20,040 --> 00:08:29,333
“Is that left eye off?” “Is all that detail where you want attention?” “Are you hiding something you don't understand behind atmosphere?”

52
00:08:29,339 --> 00:08:37,611
That observer keeps interrupting me. If you're too lenient with yourself, imagine someone you admire watching from behind.

53
00:08:37,611 --> 00:08:51,200
Don't let your hands run on their own. Draw with both the part of you that creates and the part that watches critically.

54
00:08:54,800 --> 00:08:59,527
Rule five: “Don't call it finished the moment it looks finished.”

55
00:08:59,527 --> 00:09:05,927
When I think the drawing is done, I examine it once more.

56
00:09:05,927 --> 00:09:16,896
Are the colors right? Does the eye go where I want? Is the composition right? Is the amount of detail balanced?

57
00:09:16,896 --> 00:09:33,166
Even for a close-up portrait, I often draw farther out and crop at the end so I can choose the composition after seeing the finished picture.

58
00:09:33,169 --> 00:09:43,080
Cropping closer might make the eyes more striking; pulling back might show the light better.

59
00:09:43,080 --> 00:09:52,845
This may seem to contradict rule one, but I don't think it does.

60
00:09:52,845 --> 00:10:02,683
The initial vision is a hypothesis. Once the picture is drawn, you have information you lacked at the start.

61
00:10:02,685 --> 00:10:10,845
At the end, I judge again: is the original idea better, or is what I see now better?

62
00:10:11,325 --> 00:10:18,166
Posting it on social media isn't the end. I consider it finished after analyzing the response.

63
00:10:18,173 --> 00:10:37,483
I study which pictures reach people and which get reactions. If one doesn't, I check whether it had few impressions or whether people saw it but didn't respond.

64
00:10:37,483 --> 00:10:46,683
I like analyzing results, but I no longer treat social media numbers as a score for my art or my worth.

65
00:10:46,683 --> 00:11:01,766
My online persona isn't my whole self. I think of it almost like an avatar in an illustrator life simulation game.

66
00:11:01,771 --> 00:11:11,131
I treat results as game data: “I gained experience from this picture,” “I didn't clear this stage,” or “Maybe this is my best skill.”

67
00:11:11,131 --> 00:11:23,366
Poor numbers in the game needn't hurt the person I am in real life. I still take it seriously, and enjoy it seriously.

68
00:11:23,370 --> 00:11:36,316
I see social media metrics as one source of information for what to do next, rather than a grade for myself.

69
00:11:40,203 --> 00:11:49,963
Those are the five things I keep in mind when drawing with my brain rather than just my hands. To recap:

70
00:11:49,963 --> 00:12:05,362
1. Picture the result first. 2. Understand what you draw. 3. Face what you cannot do yet. 4. Observe your own work critically. 5. Reassess the result before calling it finished.

71
00:12:05,362 --> 00:12:18,449
These rules sound stern, but I don't mean you must think through difficult theory or justify every brushstroke.

72
00:12:18,449 --> 00:12:26,782
To me, drawing with your brain means spending more time aware of what you're trying to do.

73
00:12:26,782 --> 00:12:34,942
What do I want to draw? Why this way? What can't I do yet? What do I want to learn next?

74
00:12:34,942 --> 00:12:42,400
I draw with those questions in mind. I also love losing myself in drawing without thinking.

75
00:12:42,408 --> 00:12:55,600
When practicing to improve, I value what I learn from each picture more than the number I finish.

76
00:12:55,600 --> 00:13:09,016
By understanding and doing a little more with each drawing, I can make art that communicates what I want to show, as well as improve technically.

77
00:13:09,016 --> 00:13:18,183
I still have a lot to learn, and I want to keep gaining something from each picture I draw.

78
00:13:18,183 --> 00:13:29,016
Tell me in the comments what you keep in mind when drawing. I'd love to know what you think about.

79
00:13:29,023 --> 00:13:35,716
Thank you for watching to the end. See you in the next video!
